--- CRITERION 2: three separately-reported jobs (build/vet/test, staticcheck,
race). MET.

  $ python3 ... print(list(d['jobs'].keys()))
  JOB IDS: ['test', 'staticcheck', 'race']
   job test        | name= build, vet, test | timeout-minutes= 15
   job staticcheck | name= staticcheck      | timeout-minutes= 15
   job race        | name= race detector    | timeout-minutes= 45

They are three top-level jobs with no `needs:` between them, so they report
independently; confirmed on the real PR run, where all three appear as
separate check entries with separate durations:
  $ gh pr checks 42 --repo dgallion1/simpleBudget
  build, vet, test   pass   4m11s   .../job/97050281123
  race detector      pass  14m11s   .../job/97050281036
  staticcheck        pass     28s   .../job/97050280926
Three distinct job IDs under one run ID => a lint failure and a test failure
would surface as two separate red checks, which is the split the criterion
demands.

--- CRITERION 3: explicit `go test -timeout` in BOTH test jobs, each below its
own job's timeout-minutes. MET. Numbers, as requested:

  $ grep -n 'timeout-minutes\|go test' .github/workflows/test.yml
  23:    timeout-minutes: 15                        <- job `test`
  45:      - run: go test -timeout 12m ./...        <- job `test`
  77:    timeout-minutes: 45                        <- job `race`
  93:      - run: go test -race -timeout 40m ./...  <- job `race`

  job `test`: -timeout 12m vs timeout-minutes 15  -> 12 < 15, MET
  job `race`: -timeout 40m vs timeout-minutes 45  -> 40 < 45, MET

Both are explicit (neither relies on go test's default 10m per-package cap,
which is 36da2de's subject), and both sit under their job budget, so a hung
package panics with a Go stack dump inside the job rather than being killed
opaquely by the runner. The third job (staticcheck) runs no `go test` and is
correctly out of scope for this criterion, which names "both test jobs".

Observation, not a failure: `-timeout` is a per-package cap, so 12m does not
bound the whole `./...` invocation to under the 15m job budget if several
packages are individually slow. The criterion as written asks only that each
-timeout be below its job's timeout-minutes, and it is. Measured margins on
the real run are comfortable (test job 4m11s of 15m; race job 14m11s of 45m).

